      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right that ignoring immediate capacity issues is irresponsible, and opponents have a legitimate point when schools are already at 95% capacity. However, the real argument isn't that density is inherently bad, but that current zoning laws are so restrictive they prevent the city from building the necessary infrastructure to support growth in the first place. The solution isn't to dismiss their fears, but to demand that the developer and city council commit to specific, binding infrastructure upgrades—like new bus routes or school construction—before any units are approved. This approach acknowledges their valid concerns while proving that density, when managed correctly, is the only way to solve the housing crisis without sacrificing community stability.",
